s for CTF Cryptography",slug:"rsa-attacks-ctf",description:"RSA attacks for CTF: small public exponent, weak modulus factoring, common modulus, Wiener's attack, and oracle decryption, with picoCTF challenge links.",date:"2026-04-04",tags:["cryptography","rsa","ctf-tools"],sources:[{name:"RFC 8017: PKCS #1 v2.2 RSA Cryptography Specifications",url:"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8017",publisher:"IETF"},{name:"NIST SP 800-57 Part 1 Rev. 5: Recommendation for Key Management",url:"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/SpecialPublications/NIST.SP.800-57pt1r5.pdf",publisher:"NIST"},{name:"NIST SP 800-131A Rev. 2: Transitioning the Use of Cryptographic Algorithms",url:"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/SpecialPublications/NIST.SP.800-131Ar2.pdf",publisher:"NIST"},{name:'Wiener, "Cryptanalysis of Short RSA Secret Exponents" (IEEE Trans. Inf. Theory, 1990)',url:"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/54902",publisher:"IEEE"},{name:'Boudot et al., "Comparing the difficulty of factorization and discrete logarithm" (RSA-250 record)',url:"https://eprint.iacr.org/2020/697",publisher:"IACR ePrint Archive"},{name:'Lenstra et al., "Ron was wrong, Whit is right"',url:"https://eprint.iacr.org/2012/064",publisher:"IACR ePrint Archive"},{name:'Boneh and Durfee, "Cryptanalysis of RSA with Private Key d Less than N^0.292"',url:"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/3-540-48910-X_1",publisher:"EUROCRYPT 1999"},{name:"CVE-2008-0166: Debian OpenSSL predictable random number generator",url:"https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2008-0166",publisher:"NIST NVD"},{name:"ROBOT: Return Of Bleichenbacher's Oracle Threat (USENIX Security 2018)",url:"https://robotattack.org/",publisher:"USENIX"}]},{title:"Buffer Overflow and Binary Exploitation for CTF",seoTitle:"Buffer Overflow for CTF",slug:"buffer-overflow-binary-exploitation-ctf",description:"Buffer overflow and pwn for CTF: stack overflows, ret2win, format strings, heap exploits, and ASLR/PIE bypass, with a picoCTF challenge link for each techni