ts, increment IDs, and force-browse to admin pages.",date:"2026-06-22",sources:[{name:"OWASP Top 10 2021: A01 Broken Access Control",url:"https://owasp.org/Top10/A01_2021-Broken_Access_Control/",publisher:"OWASP Foundation"},{name:"CWE-639: Authorization Bypass Through User-Controlled Key",url:"https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/639.html",publisher:"MITRE"},{name:"CWE-863: Incorrect Authorization",url:"https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/863.html",publisher:"MITRE"},{name:"RFC 6265: HTTP State Management Mechanism",url:"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6265",publisher:"IETF"},{name:"RFC 9110: HTTP Semantics",url:"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110",publisher:"IETF"}],tags:["web","access-control","idor","authentication","cookies","ctf-tools"]},{title:"Padding Oracle and CBC Bit-Flipping Attacks for CTF",seoTitle:"Padding Oracle and CBC Attacks for CTF",slug:"padding-oracle-cbc-ctf",description:"Decrypt AES-CBC with no key. The padding oracle leaks plaintext one byte at a time, and CBC bit-flipping forges it. Working Python, padbuster, and the math.",date:"2026-06-23",sources:[{name:"NIST SP 800-38A: Recommendation for Block Cipher Modes of Operation",url:"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/Legacy/SP/nistspecialpublication800-38a.pdf",publisher:"NIST"},{name:"RFC 5652: Cryptographic Message Syntax (PKCS #7 padding)",url:"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc5652",publisher:"IETF"},{name:'Vaudenay, "Security Flaws Induced by CBC Padding" (EUROCRYPT 2002)',url:"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/3-540-46035-7_35",publisher:"EUROCRYPT"},{name:"CVE-2010-3332: ASP.NET padding oracle",url:"https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2010-3332",publisher:"NIST NVD"},{name:"CVE-2014-3566 (POODLE)",url:"https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2014-3566",publisher:"NIST NVD"}],tags:["cryptography","crypto","aes-cbc","padding-oracle","bit-flipping","ctf-tools"]},{title:"Insecure Randomness for CTF: Predicting PRNGs and the Mersenne Twister",seoTitle:"Insecure Randomness and PRNGs for CTF",slug:"insecure-randomness-ctf",description:"Predict Python's random, crack the Mersenne Twister with randcrack from 624 outputs, brute a time() seed, and recover an LCG. Working code for CTF guessing games.",date:"2026-06-24",sources:[{name:"NIST SP 800-90A Rev. 1: Recommendation for Random Number Generation Using Deterministic Random Bit Generators",url:"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/SpecialPublications/NIST.SP.800-90Ar1.pdf",publisher:"NIST"},{name:"CWE-330: Use of Insufficiently Random Values",url:"https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/330.html",publisher:"MITRE"},{name:"CWE-338: Use of Cryptographically Weak Pseudo-Random Number Generator",url:"https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/338.html",publisher:"MITRE"},{name:"Python random module documentation (Mersenne Twister)",url:"https://docs.python.org/3/library/random.html",publisher:"Python Software Foundation"},{name:"Python secrets module documentation",url:"https://docs.python.org/3/library/secrets.html",publisher:"Python Software Foundation"},{name:"CVE-2008-0166: Debian OpenSSL predictable random number generator",url:"https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2008-0166",publisher:"NIST NVD"}],tags:["cryptography","prng","mersenne-twister","randcrack","lcg","ctf-tools"]},{title:"Hash Length Extension Attacks for CTF: Forging a MAC Without the Secret",seoTitle:"Hash Length Extension Attacks for CTF",slug:"hash-length-extension-ctf",description:"Hash length extension attacks: forge a valid MAC on hash(secret + message) without the secret. How Merkle-Damgard leaks state, plus hashpump and hash_extender.",date:"2026-06-25",sources:[{name:"FIPS 180-4: Secure Hash Standard",url:"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/FIPS/NIST.FIPS.180-4.pdf",publisher:"NIST"},{name:"RFC 2104: HMAC, Keyed-Hashing for Message Authentication",url:"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc2104",publisher:"IETF"},{name:"RFC 1321: The MD5 Message-Digest Algorithm",url:"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1321",publisher:"IETF"},{nam